13"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-13T09:32:56-08:00","id":71380,"title":"","content":"I'm planning a game set in Medieval Sweden and this info is very welcome in case any of my character would decide to try it over the waters. With you permission I will outright steal all of it. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/970-gurkhal","name":"Gurkhal","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"863"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-13T10:47:03-08:00","id":71424,"title":"","content":"Blue Fox : Thank you for your maps. I will try to upgrade the map i posted before to mach medieval setting.<br /> <br /> Ryoga84 : Excellent note about Tzimice Methuselah <a href=\"http://whitewolf.wikia.com/wiki/Byelobog\" target=\"_blank\">Byelobog</a> in Baltic lands. Thank you very much!<br /> <br /> Gurkhal : Nice to hear. Use everything you find useful. More will follow.<br /> <br /> I updated previous post with map of Riga. Have a look.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2148,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2148-lachplesis","user":"Lachplesis","date":". 02-13-2014, 02:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2148-lachplesis","name":"Lachplesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"21"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-13T12:39:06-08:00","id":71522,"title":"","content":"You should Remember Lachplesis that each map of the era is an approximate because at the medieval era the boundaries were more or less fluid and the controlled area count was more based on villages and holds under your tribute &amp; yoke than any hard and fast border which, when marked to actual maps during treaties; usually were laid out on the landscape on foot which was very much prone to errors (lack of compass &amp; accurate maps) and up to individual border marking groups whims when they were marked into wilderness. There are few cases where the markers of Russian and Swedish delegations just got bored and went home after trekking at the Taiga for months on end and just agreed that -yeah that's it we're done, the borders go from there to there between those certain landmarks here and here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1700-blue-fox","name":"Blue Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1700_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-18T02:07:53-08:00","id":74171,"title":"","content":"Great thread!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/629-anda","name":"anda","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar629_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"156"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-09T12:26:21-07:00","id":87656,"title":"","content":"Hello! anda cheers!<br /> <br /> Here is map of Lithuanian medieval settlement - Kernave.<br /> <br /> Posible fiction :<br /> There is some speculations that it name meaning is - &quot;kjer naave&quot;, or in english &quot;catch death&quot;. Maybe its realated to trap used against enemy in siege or raid.<br /> <br /> Usualy Livonian and Lithuanian pagan setlements can be divided in two groups:<br /> <b>A</b>) Single castle based on hill and settlement around ( clasical and used all around medieval world using any natural advantages like rivers, clifs etc.)<br /> <br /> <b>B</b>) Scattered forts and castles next to settlement (can be seen here in Kernave exsample also using natural advantages but in larger tactical scale)<br /> From first look it can be seen not smart way for defense...but even Maori had stunning victories while defending similar &quot;Pa&quot; forts against superior British army.<br /> All are conected with underground passages giving posibility to reposition forces, lure enemy to seemingly weaker positions, attack from unexpected direction, creating crosfire and of course to retreat. Its worth to mention that pagans was rather leaving doomed positions than face certain death. Livonian and Lithuanian lands was full of small castles and forts and was easy to rebuild. This kept enemy bizzy taking each defensive position while defenders could regroup and call for help. Crusaders soon started to use scorched land tactics and burned grainfields. Genocide and lack of food in long term turned out to be more devastating for pagans than some fort loses.<br /> <br /> For DA roleplay scattered settlements could give easy navigation for players to encounter various sub factions, characters or services or claim some part for themselves. (For exsample Chieftan keep, Warrior keep, Priest keep, nobility settlement and simple folk settlement. etc.)<br /> <img src=\"http://i.imgur.com/eaV4OBl.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/> The area of Kernav\u0117 was sparingly inhabited at the end of the <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paleolithic\" target=\"_blank\">Paleolithic</a> era, with the number of settlements significantly increasing in the <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mesolithic\" target=\"_blank\">Mesolithic</a> and <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neolithic\" target=\"_blank\">Neolithic</a> eras.<br /> The town was first mentioned in 1279, when, as the capital of the Grand Duke <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Traidenis\" target=\"_blank\">Traidenis</a>, it was besieged by the <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teutonic_Knights\" target=\"_blank\">Teutonic Knights</a>. In 1390, during the <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lithuanian_Civil_War_%281389%E2%80%931392%29\" target=\"_blank\">Lithuanian Civil War (1389\u20131392)</a>, the knights burned the town and its buildings, including the castle. After this raid, the town wasn't rebuilt, and the remaining residents moved to the top of the hill instead of staying in the valley.<br /> <br /> Kernav\u0117 has the oldest known <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K%C5%ABlgrinda\" target=\"_blank\">medgrinda</a>, a secret underwater road paved with wood. The road was used for defense and dates from the 4\u20137th centuries.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2148,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2148-lachplesis","user":"Lachplesis","date":". 08-12-2014, 11:35 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2148-lachplesis","name":"Lachplesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"21"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-10T03:40:00-07:00","id":88049,"title":"","content":"Helou<br /> <br /> On Wikipedia: subject Balts ther is good map<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balts\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Balts.svg/1280px-Balts.svg.png?utm_sour